The initial value for next\_out needs some explaining. Since the queue is initially empty, there is no next element to remove, so next\_out is invalid. Setting next\_out == next\_in is a special case that indicates that the queue is empty, so we can write:

```c
int queue_empty(Queue *queue)
{
    return (queue->next_in == queue->next_out);
}
```

Now we can add elements to the queue using queue\_push:

```c
void queue_push(Queue *queue, int item) {
    if (queue_full(queue)) {
        perror_exit("queue is full");
    }

    queue->array[queue->next_in] = item;
