Now that we have the basic approach worked out, I'd like to make a few tweaks for efficiency's sake. I'll replace the capturing parentheses with atomic grouping (there's no need to capture, nor to backtrack), and once that's done, I can change \$\left[\hat{^()}\right]\$to\$\left[\hat{^()}\right] +\$ for added efficiency. (Don't make this change without using atomic grouping, or you'll set yourself up for a neverending match 226.)

Finally, I'd like to move the  and  so that they directly surround the dynamic regex. This way, the dynamic regex construct isn't invoked by the engine until it's sure that there's something for it to match. Here's the revised version:

$$
\$ \text {LevelN} = \mathrm{qr/} (\ref {e q : 1}) [ ^ {()} ] + | \backslash (\ref {e q : 1} (\ref {e q : 1} (\ref {e q : 1} (\ref {e q : 1})) * / x;
$$

Since this no longer has outer \$\left[\cdots\right]\$, we need to include them ourselves when invoking \$LevelN.

As a side effect of that, we have the flexibility to apply it where there may be sets of parentheses, not just where there are sets of parentheses:

if (\$text =~ m/\b( \w+ \$LevelN\$ ))/x) {
    print "found function call: \$1\n";
}
if (not \$text =~ m/^ \$LevelN \$/x) {
    print "mismatched parentheses!\n";
}

