```python

This is a bit more cumbersome, but documents clearly where exactly the object is coming from. Again, it's a matter of style.

## Matching Nested Constructs

Microsoft has included an interesting innovation for matching balanced constructs (historically, something not possible with a regular expression). It's not particularly easy to understand—this section is short, but be warned, it is very dense.

It's easiest to understand with an example, so I'll start with one:

Dim R As Regex = New Regex(" \ (
"    (?&gt;          " &amp;
"       [^()]+        " &amp;
"     |               " &amp;
"       $(?&lt;DEPTH&gt;)      " &amp;
"     |               " &amp;
"$ (?&lt;-DEPTH&gt;)  " &amp;
"   )*               " &amp;
"   (? (DEPTH) (!))      " &amp;
" \)                       ", _
RegexOptions.IgnorePatternWhitespace)

This matches the first properly-paired nested set of parentheses, such as the underlined portion of ‘before (nope (yes (here) okay) after’. The first parenthesis isn’t matched because it has no associated closing parenthesis.

Here's the super-short overview of how it works:

1. With each ‘(’ matched, 「(?&lt;DEPTH&gt;)」 adds one to the regex’s idea of how deep the parentheses are currently nested (at least, nested beyond the initial \ (j at the start of the regex).

2. With each ‘)’ matched,  $\left[\left( ?<-DEPTH >\right) \right]$  subtracts one from that depth.

3. 「(? (DEPTH) (? !))」 ensures that the depth is zero before allowing the final literal \ to match.

This works because the engine's backtracking stack keeps track of successfully-matched groupings. $\left(?\text{DEPTH}\right)$ is just a named-capture version of $($\), which is always successful. Since it has been placed immediately after $\backslash ($, its success (which remains on the stack until removed) is used as a marker for counting opening parentheses.

Thus, the number of successful 'DEPTH' groupings matched so far is maintained on the backtracking stack. We want to subtract from that whenever a closing parentheses is found. That's accomplished by .NET's special [(?<-DEPTH>) construct,

which removes the most recent “successful DEPTH” notation from the stack. If it turns out that there aren’t any, the  $\left[\left(?\leftarrow\text{DEPTH}\right)\right]$  itself fails, thereby disallowing the regex from over-matching an extra closing parenthesis.

